# Europe batch 3 (1769-1848): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"eu176954": dict(
 bluf="Glasgow instrument maker James Watt set out to stop the Newcomen engine wasting most of its coal reheating the cylinder on every stroke. His separate condenser, patented in 1769, cut fuel use by about three-quarters, and with Birmingham manufacturer Matthew Boulton he built engines that could work far from the pits and later turn wheels. Steam freed factories from rivers and muscle, and the large-scale burning of fossil fuels began.",
 now="Fossil-fuel-powered growth created modern prosperity and began the carbon emissions behind climate change; today's energy transition is an attempt to replace what Watt's engine started.",
 story="English ironmonger Thomas Newcomen's atmospheric engine had pumped water from mines since 1712 and wasted most of its fuel reheating the cylinder each stroke. In 1765, repairing a model of it for Glasgow University, Glasgow instrument maker James Watt saw the waste and designed a separate condenser, patented in 1769, which cut coal use by about three-quarters. Birmingham manufacturer Matthew Boulton went into partnership with him and the firm sold engines from 1775. Later engines turned wheels and drove mills and factories.",
 why="Britain had cheap coal and high wages, which rewarded a machine that saved labour. Its mines needed better pumps. Patent law and skilled instrument makers let an inventor profit from an improvement.",
 then="Rotary steam engines powered textile mills and ironworks and later railways and steamships, which freed factories from rivers. British output and productivity grew. Industrialisation spread to Europe and North America in the 1800s.",
 fact="Watt coined the term 'horsepower' to sell engines; the unit 'watt' is named after him."),
"d-cromford": dict(
 bluf="Lancashire inventor Richard Arkwright wanted to run his water frame, which spun strong cotton yarn, at a scale no cottage could match. The mill he opened at Cromford in 1771 put machines in one building and hundreds of workers on fixed shifts and wages, day and night. The factory system, with its discipline, wage labour and continuous production, spread from Cromford across Britain and then the world.",
 now="Cromford was the first factory in the modern sense, a water-powered mill with hundreds of workers on shifts; the factory system of discipline, wage labour and mass production spread from it across Britain and the world.",
 story="Demand for cotton cloth was rising and hand spinners could supply neither the quantity nor the strength of yarn the weavers wanted. Lancashire inventor Richard Arkwright, who had patented the water frame in 1769, opened a mill on the River Derwent at Cromford in 1771 that ran day and night in shifts. He built houses for his workers, many of them children.",
 why="Demand for cotton cloth was rising faster than hand spinners could meet it. The water frame could spin strong yarn and needed a river and a building rather than a cottage.",
 then="Hundreds of similar mills spread across Britain. Derbyshire-trained mechanic Samuel Slater, a former Arkwright apprentice, carried the design to the United States in 1790.",
 fact="Cromford is part of the Derwent Valley Mills World Heritage site."),
"eu177255": dict(
 bluf="Prussian King Frederick II, Russian Empress Catherine II and Habsburg Empress Maria Theresa each wanted territory and a settled balance among themselves, and found it easier to take Poland's land than to fight each other for it. In 1772 they annexed about a third of Poland-Lithuania's territory without a war. Two more partitions in 1793 and 1795 erased the state for 123 years, and the rule that a weak state between strong neighbours can be removed by agreement among them entered European politics.",
 now="The partitions fixed the Polish reading of its geography: a state between Russia and Germany survives only inside an alliance, which underlies Poland's commitment to NATO since 1999 and its support for Ukraine since 2022.",
 story="Poland-Lithuania had elected kings and a parliament in which any one member could veto legislation, and Russian troops had been fighting a noble revolt against Russian influence, the Bar Confederation, since 1768. In 1772 Russian Empress Catherine II, Prussian King Frederick II and Habsburg Empress Maria Theresa agreed to annex about a third of Poland-Lithuania's territory. Prussia took most of Royal Prussia, Austria took Galicia and Russia the eastern provinces. The Polish parliament ratified the loss in 1773 under duress. Further partitions in 1793 and 1795 divided the rest, and Poland vanished from the map until 1918.",
 why="Elected kings and a parliament that any one member could veto left Poland unable to reform or to resist. Prussia wanted the land joining Brandenburg to East Prussia. Russia and Austria preferred to compensate each other in Poland rather than fight over the Ottoman lands Russia was conquering.",
 then="Poland's reforming constitution of 3 May 1791 provoked Russian intervention and the second partition in 1793. A rising under Tadeusz Kościuszko in 1794 was crushed, and the third partition of 1795 ended the state. Poland had no state of its own for 123 years.",
 fact="Maria Theresa wept over the partition and signed anyway; Frederick II remarked that 'she wept, but she took'."),
"eu177656": dict(
 bluf="Scottish philosopher Adam Smith set out to explain how nations grow rich and to refute the mercantilist doctrine that wealth means hoarding gold through trade surpluses. He argued that wealth comes from productive labour, the division of labour and free exchange, and that self-interest in competitive markets tends to serve the public. The book founded modern economics and supplied the case for free trade that Britain adopted in the 1800s.",
 now="Modern economics starts here. Today's renewed fights over tariffs, industrial policy and free trade are still argued in Smith's terms.",
 story="Scottish philosopher Adam Smith published 'An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations' in 1776. He argued that dividing labour into specialised tasks multiplies output, and that individuals pursuing their own interest in competitive markets are led as by an 'invisible hand' to benefit society. He attacked mercantilism, monopolies such as the East India Company and trade barriers. Governments, he wrote, must provide defence, justice and public works.",
 why="Smith wanted to explain why some nations were growing richer than others. Mercantilist doctrine held that wealth meant gold earned by trade surpluses, and he set out to refute it.",
 then="Britain moved toward free trade in the 1800s and repealed the Corn Laws in 1846. London stockbroker David Ricardo and English clergyman Thomas Malthus built on Smith; German philosopher Karl Marx built against him.",
 fact="The book appeared in 1776, the year of the American Declaration of Independence; its opening example is a pin factory where ten men make 48,000 pins a day."),
"d-france1778": dict(
 bluf="French King Louis XVI and his foreign minister the Comte de Vergennes wanted revenge for the loss of Canada in 1763 and a weaker Britain; American envoy Benjamin Franklin wanted money, troops and a navy. The treaties of February 1778 delivered all three, and a French fleet trapped British general Lord Cornwallis at Yorktown in 1781. The United States won its independence, and the debt France took on to pay for it helped bring on its own revolution in 1789.",
 now="The cost of the war deepened the French crown's debts and produced the fiscal crisis that forced Louis XVI to summon the Estates-General in 1789.",
 story="The American colonies had been at war with Britain since 1775 and needed a European ally. American envoy Benjamin Franklin negotiated in Paris from late 1776. News of the British surrender at Saratoga in October 1777 reached Paris in December. On 6 February 1778 France and the United States signed treaties of commerce and alliance, and French money, soldiers and above all the navy followed.",
 why="France wanted revenge for the loss of Canada in the Seven Years' War and to weaken Britain. Saratoga showed that the Americans could win and were worth backing.",
 trig="The American victory at Saratoga in October 1777.",
 then="Britain and France were at war by the summer of 1778 and Spain joined France in 1779. A French fleet under Admiral de Grasse closed Chesapeake Bay, and the Franco-American army took British general Lord Cornwallis's surrender at Yorktown in October 1781. France's war debts fed the crisis of 1788-89.",
 fact="Franklin went about the French court in a fur cap and without a wig, and Paris made a fashion of it."),
"d-abolsociety": dict(
 bluf="Campaigner Thomas Clarkson, lawyer Granville Sharp and nine Quakers founded a society in London to turn British opinion against the slave trade and to get Parliament to ban it. They invented the pressure campaign: evidence gathered from sailors and surgeons, mass petitions, a sugar boycott and a printed image of a packed slave ship. Parliament abolished the British trade in 1807, and the society's methods became the model for every later movement.",
 now="The society's method, evidence plus petitions plus boycott plus emblem, is the template every pressure campaign since has followed, from Chartism to the climate movement.",
 story="The Society for Effecting the Abolition of the Slave Trade was founded on 22 May 1787 by nine Quakers and three Anglicans, among them campaigner Thomas Clarkson and lawyer Granville Sharp. Clarkson rode the country collecting testimony from sailors and ships' surgeons. The society printed the diagram of the slave ship Brookes and organised petitions and a sugar boycott. MP William Wilberforce led the cause in the Commons.",
 why="Quakers had opposed slavery for decades. Evangelical and Enlightenment criticism of the trade was growing among the wider public.",
 then="Wilberforce's first abolition bill failed in 1791 and the war with France stalled the campaign through the 1790s. Parliament banned the trade in 1807 after 20 years of campaigning.",
 fact="Potter Josiah Wedgwood made the campaign's emblem, a kneeling man in chains asking 'Am I Not a Man and a Brother?'"),
"d-rightsofman": dict(
 bluf="The National Assembly wanted to set out the principles of the new order before it wrote France's constitution. On 26 August 1789 it adopted a declaration of natural, universal rights and the sovereignty of the nation, which replaced privilege and divine right as the basis of French law. The text became the model for rights declarations worldwide, and its silence on women and the enslaved marked the limits of 'universal' as the Assembly used it.",
 now="It is part of France's constitution today and inspired the 1948 Universal Declaration of Human Rights.",
 story="The National Assembly had abolished feudal privileges on the night of 4 August 1789. On 26 August it adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen. Its 17 articles listed natural rights to liberty, property, security and resistance to oppression, with free expression, religious tolerance and equality before the law. French deputy and general the Marquis de Lafayette had drafted an early version with advice from American minister Thomas Jefferson.",
 why="The Assembly wanted principles agreed before it wrote a constitution. Deputies had read the American declarations and the Enlightenment case for natural rights.",
 then="The Constitution of 1791 placed the Declaration at its head. Playwright Olympe de Gouges answered in 1791 with a Declaration of the Rights of Woman; she was guillotined in 1793.",
 fact="The Declaration was adopted as the preamble to a constitution that took another two years to write."),
"d-womensmarch": dict(
 bluf="Parisian market women marched on Versailles to get bread and to bring the king back to Paris, where the people could watch him. They got both: French King Louis XVI accepted the August decrees and the Declaration of Rights, and the next day the royal family was moved to the Tuileries. The king lived under the Paris crowd from then on, and the capital's streets rather than the court set the Revolution's pace.",
 now="The march moved the king from Versailles to Paris and put him under the crowd's control, the step from which the Revolution's radical phase followed; it showed that women's collective action could change politics, which the Revolution's leaders then suppressed.",
 story="Bread was short and dear in Paris in the autumn of 1789, and reports said the king's bodyguards had insulted the tricolour at a banquet in Versailles. On 5 October 1789 a crowd of thousands of women marched 20 km to Versailles; the Marquis de Lafayette followed with the National Guard. Early on 6 October part of the crowd broke into the palace and killed guards. French King Louis XVI, Queen Marie Antoinette and their children were taken to the Tuileries Palace in Paris that day.",
 why="Bread prices came first. Rumours that the court was plotting against the Revolution came second.",
 trig="The reported insult to the tricolour at the royal bodyguards' banquet on 1 October 1789.",
 then="The king lived in the Tuileries as a prisoner in effect. The National Assembly moved to Paris within days, where the clubs and crowds could press it.",
 fact="The crowd escorted the royal family back to Paris chanting that it was bringing 'the baker, the baker's wife and the baker's boy'."),
"eu178957": dict(
 bluf="French King Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General to approve new taxes for a bankrupt state; the Third Estate used it to claim sovereignty for the nation. Within three months the Bastille had fallen, feudal privilege was abolished and the Rights of Man proclaimed; within four years France was a republic at war with Europe and the king was dead. Popular sovereignty, the nation and the language of left and right entered politics, and the Terror set the question of whether revolutions must turn violent.",
 now="Liberty, equality and nationalism, the language of 'left' and 'right', declarations of human rights, the secular state, and the argument over whether revolutions must turn violent all trace back here.",
 story="The state was bankrupt after the American war and bread prices had risen after the failed harvest of 1788. French King Louis XVI called the Estates-General, which met in May 1789 for the first time since 1614. The Third Estate declared itself a National Assembly on 17 June, Parisians stormed the Bastille on 14 July, and the Assembly abolished feudal privileges on 4 August and issued the Declaration of the Rights of Man on 26 August. Over the next decade France became a republic, executed the king, fought most of Europe and lived through the Terror.",
 why="The state could not tax its privileged nobles and clergy and was bankrupt after its wars, including its aid to the Americans. An educated middle class was shut out of power. Failed harvests in 1788 put bread beyond the poor, and Enlightenment ideas supplied the vocabulary.",
 trig="Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General to approve new taxes, during the bread crisis of 1788-89.",
 then="France abolished feudalism, seized church lands, became a republic in September 1792 and executed Louis XVI in January 1793. War with Europe's monarchies and revolt in the Vendée brought the Terror of 1793-94. General Napoleon Bonaparte took power in 1799 and carried revolutionary legal reforms across Europe.",
 fact="When the Bastille was stormed it held only seven prisoners."),
"d-varennes": dict(
 bluf="French King Louis XVI and Queen Marie Antoinette fled Paris in disguise to reach loyal troops near the frontier and recover the king's powers from outside the capital. A postmaster recognised him on the road and the family was stopped at Varennes and brought back under guard. The king was now a suspected traitor; the republican movement grew from a fringe into a force within months, and the monarchy fell fourteen months later.",
 now="The flight proved the king would undo the Revolution if he could; the republican movement, a fringe before, became a force within months, and the monarchy fell fourteen months later.",
 story="On the night of 20 June 1791 French King Louis XVI, Queen Marie Antoinette and their children slipped out of the Tuileries disguised as the household of a Russian baroness, heading for royalist troops under General Bouillé near Montmédy. Postmaster Jean-Baptiste Drouet recognised the king at Sainte-Menehould and rode ahead. The family was stopped at Varennes on 21 June and brought back to Paris under guard on 25 June.",
 why="Louis no longer accepted the Revolution and hoped to recover his power from outside Paris. The Civil Constitution of the Clergy, which he had signed under protest, weighed on his conscience.",
 then="The Assembly suspended the king and then restored him on the fiction that he had been abducted. A republican petition on the Champ de Mars on 17 July 1791 ended with the National Guard firing on the crowd. The monarchy was abolished in September 1792.",
 fact="The king travelled as a valet named Durand, and the heavy coach ran hours behind schedule."),
"eu179358": dict(
 bluf="The National Convention tried French King Louis XVI to make the republic irreversible and to settle whether a king could be punished by his people. It found him guilty of conspiring against liberty almost unanimously and voted for death by a narrow margin, and he was guillotined on 21 January 1793. The execution turned the war into one against every monarchy in Europe, and France's split between republicans and royalists ran through its politics for a century.",
 now="It made putting a head of state on trial thinkable, and France's split between republicans and royalists ran through its politics for another century.",
 story="The monarchy was overthrown on 10 August 1792 and the republic proclaimed in September. The National Convention tried 'Citizen Louis Capet' for treason in December 1792 and January 1793. Deputies voted almost unanimously that he was guilty; the death sentence passed by a narrow majority. He was guillotined in the Place de la Révolution on 21 January 1793. Queen Marie Antoinette followed in October, as the Terror began.",
 why="The flight to Varennes in 1791 and the king's correspondence with France's enemies, found in an iron cupboard at the Tuileries in November 1792, destroyed trust in him. The Montagnards wanted the Convention bound to the republic by the king's blood.",
 then="Britain, Spain and the Dutch Republic joined the war against France within weeks. Revolt broke out in the Vendée in March 1793. The Revolution, at war with much of Europe and facing revolt at home, turned to the Terror.",
 fact="Louis began a speech from the scaffold declaring his innocence; the officer in command ordered the drums to drown him out."),
"eu179358b": dict(
 bluf="The Committee of Public Safety, with deputy Maximilien Robespierre its leading voice, governed by emergency decree to win a war on every frontier and crush revolt at home. It won the war and crushed the revolts, at the cost of about 17,000 executions after trial and many more deaths in prison and in the Vendée. 'Terror' entered politics as a word for government by fear, and the episode became the standard warning against revolution.",
 now="'Terror' entered politics as a word for government by fear, and the case that a revolution for liberty ends in dictatorship has been argued from it ever since.",
 story="By the spring of 1793 France was at war with Austria, Prussia, Britain, Spain and the Dutch Republic and in revolt in the Vendée and the federalist cities of Lyon, Marseille and Toulon. The National Convention gave the Committee of Public Safety executive power, and deputy Maximilien Robespierre joined it in July 1793. The Law of Suspects of September 1793 filled the prisons. About 17,000 people were executed after trial, and many more died in prison or in the crushing of the Vendée. The Terror ended when Robespierre himself was guillotined on 28 July 1794.",
 why="War and fear of invasion came first. Revolt in the Vendée and the federalist cities came second. Jacobin doctrine, which held virtue powerless without terror, supplied the justification.",
 then="The Convention dismantled the Terror's machinery after Thermidor, and a White Terror struck Jacobins in the south in 1795. The Directory governed from 1795 until General Napoleon Bonaparte took power in 1799.",
 fact="Robespierre had argued in 1791 for abolishing the death penalty."),
"d-thermidor": dict(
 bluf="Deputies of the National Convention who feared they were next on deputy Maximilien Robespierre's list combined to bring him down. They shouted him down on 9 Thermidor, 27 July 1794, ordered his arrest, and guillotined him and about 20 followers the next day. The Terror ended, the Revolution turned back toward order, and 'Thermidor' became the name for that turn in every later revolution.",
 now="'Thermidor' became shorthand for the moment a revolution turns back toward moderation; Leon Trotsky applied it to Stalin's Russia in the 1930s.",
 story="French armies had won at Fleurus on 26 June 1794 and the threat of invasion had passed, yet executions in Paris rose. Deputy Maximilien Robespierre told the Convention on 8 Thermidor that unnamed traitors sat among them. On 9 Thermidor, 27 July 1794, deputies shouted him down and voted his arrest. The Paris Commune freed him; troops loyal to the Convention retook the Hôtel de Ville that night. He and about 20 followers were guillotined on 28 July.",
 why="The Terror had grown after the military crisis eased and no deputy felt safe. Robespierre's threat of an unnamed purge united his rivals on the committees with the moderates.",
 then="The Convention cut the Revolutionary Tribunal's powers and closed the Jacobin Club in November 1794. The Directory, a more conservative regime, took power in 1795.",
 fact="Robespierre's jaw had been shattered by a bullet the night before his execution, and the executioner tore off the bandage on the scaffold."),
"eu179959": dict(
 bluf="General Napoleon Bonaparte and Director Emmanuel-Joseph Sieyès plotted to replace a Directory that was corrupt, unpopular and losing the war with a stronger executive. When the Council of Five Hundred resisted on 19 Brumaire, Bonaparte's soldiers cleared the chamber, and a new constitution made him First Consul. Ten years of experiment in representative government ended; he kept the Revolution's civil gains and ended its political freedom, a template for authoritarian rule with popular backing.",
 now="The coup fixed the pattern of the plebiscitary strongman, ratified by vote in 1800 and copied from Louis-Napoleon in 1851 to the generals of the 20th century: order and civil equality in exchange for political freedom.",
 story="The Directory had governed since 1795 through purges and annulled elections and in 1799 was losing the war against the Second Coalition. General Napoleon Bonaparte returned from Egypt in October 1799 to a hero's welcome. On 18 Brumaire, 9 November, the councils moved to Saint-Cloud on the pretext of a Jacobin plot. On 19 Brumaire the Council of Five Hundred resisted, and soldiers called in by Bonaparte's brother Lucien, the council's president, cleared the chamber. A new constitution in December made Bonaparte First Consul.",
 why="The Directory was corrupt, unpopular and losing the war. Director Emmanuel-Joseph Sieyès wanted a new constitution and needed a general; Bonaparte had the reputation.",
 trig="Bonaparte's return from Egypt in October 1799.",
 then="A plebiscite approved the constitution in 1800. Bonaparte made peace with Austria in 1801 and Britain in 1802, became consul for life in 1802 and emperor in 1804. His wars redrew the map of Europe until 1815.",
 fact="Bonaparte lost his nerve before the shouting deputies; his brother Lucien saved the coup by calling in the troops."),
"d-civilcode": dict(
 bluf="First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte wanted one law for one nation in place of the hundreds of local customs that had survived the Revolution. A commission of four jurists led by Jean-Étienne-Marie Portalis produced it, and the Civil Code took effect on 21 March 1804: equality before the law, secure property, no feudal privilege, and the authority of fathers and husbands written in. It followed his armies across Europe and is the basis of civil law from Belgium to Latin America.",
 now="Civil law in countries from Belgium to much of Latin America still rests on it.",
 story="Revolutionary France had abolished feudal privilege yet kept hundreds of local legal customs, Roman law in the south and customary law in the north. First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte appointed a commission of four jurists in 1800, with Jean-Étienne-Marie Portalis its leading drafter, and attended many of the Council of State sessions that reviewed its work. The Code was enacted on 21 March 1804. It confirmed equality before the law and secure property and strengthened the power of fathers and husbands over women and children.",
 why="One nation needed one law. Bonaparte wanted the Revolution's gains in property fixed and the family put under paternal authority.",
 then="Napoleon's conquests carried the code to Italy, the Low Countries and western Germany. It was renamed the Code Napoléon in 1807.",
 fact="On St Helena Napoleon said that Waterloo would wipe out the memory of his forty victories and that his Civil Code would live for ever."),
"eu180460": dict(
 bluf="First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte wanted a hereditary throne to secure his rule and his reforms against assassination and a Bourbon return. He had the Senate proclaim him emperor in May 1804, won a plebiscite, and on 2 December crowned himself at Notre-Dame with Pope Pius VII looking on. The republic became a hereditary empire that kept the Revolution's civil equality, and a ruler who claimed power from the nation and the army rather than from the Church wore a crown.",
 now="Napoleon is the model of a ruler who uses plebiscites and popular appeal to justify concentrated power; the pattern, named Bonapartism, runs from Louis-Napoleon in 1851 to the strongmen of the 20th century.",
 story="A royalist plot to kill First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte was uncovered early in 1804, and the Duc d'Enghien was shot in March. The Senate proclaimed Napoleon Emperor of the French on 18 May, and a plebiscite endorsed the hereditary title. On 2 December 1804 at Notre-Dame, with Pope Pius VII present, he placed the crown on his own head and then crowned Josephine.",
 why="Napoleon wanted a hereditary throne to secure his rule and his reforms. The plot of 1804 showed that his death would leave France without a succession.",
 then="His armies carried the Civil Code and the abolition of feudal privilege across Europe. His wars provoked national resistance in Spain from 1808, in Germany and in Russia.",
 fact="Napoleon was about average height for a Frenchman of his time; British cartoons made him tiny."),
"eu180561": dict(
 bluf="Emperor Napoleon had spent 1805 trying to win the Channel for an invasion of Britain; British Vice-Admiral Horatio Nelson sailed to destroy the combined French and Spanish fleet when it left Cádiz. On 21 October 1805 his 27 ships cut the line of 33 in two columns and took or destroyed 22 without losing one; Nelson died of a marksman's bullet during the action. Napoleon gave up invasion for blockade, and Britain held command of the sea for a century.",
 now="Britain's century of naval dominance after Trafalgar underpinned its global empire and the free-trade order it built.",
 story="Napoleon's plan needed the French and Spanish fleets to hold the Channel long enough for his army to cross. French Vice-Admiral Pierre de Villeneuve's combined fleet took shelter in Cádiz instead, and Napoleon marched his army to Austria. Ordered to sea in October, Villeneuve met British Vice-Admiral Horatio Nelson off Cape Trafalgar on 21 October 1805; Nelson's 27 ships attacked the 33 in two columns and broke the line. No British ship was lost and 22 of the enemy were. Nelson was shot by a French marksman and died during the battle.",
 why="Napoleon needed control of the Channel to invade Britain and had ordered the combined fleet to fight. Nelson's two columns gambled on close action against a fleet with slower gunnery.",
 then="Napoleon turned to economic warfare, the Continental System, decreed at Berlin in 1806. A storm after the battle sank most of the captured ships.",
 fact="Nelson's body was brought home preserved in a cask of brandy."),
"eu180561b": dict(
 bluf="Emperor Napoleon wanted to destroy the Austro-Russian army before Prussia joined the Third Coalition; Russian Tsar Alexander I and Austrian Emperor Francis II wanted to crush him far from France. On 2 December 1805 Napoleon drew their attack onto his weakened right and smashed their centre from the Pratzen Heights. Austria made peace and lost territory, the coalition dissolved, and within eight months the Holy Roman Empire was gone and western Germany reorganised under French protection.",
 now="The dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire in 1806 and the consolidation of the German states that followed from Austerlitz set the map on which Prussia united Germany in 1871.",
 story="Britain, Austria and Russia formed the Third Coalition in 1805. Napoleon marched the Grande Armée from the Channel to the Danube, forced an Austrian army to surrender at Ulm in October and took Vienna. On 2 December 1805, a year to the day after his coronation, he met the Austro-Russian army under Russian Tsar Alexander I and Austrian Emperor Francis II at Austerlitz in Moravia. He lured them into attacking his deliberately weakened right flank, then smashed their centre from the Pratzen Heights.",
 why="The allies attacked before Prussia could join them, confident in their numbers. Napoleon had chosen the ground and read their plan.",
 then="Austria signed the Treaty of Pressburg on 26 December 1805 and lost Venetia, Tyrol and its lands in Swabia. Russia withdrew. Napoleon reorganised western Germany into the Confederation of the Rhine in July 1806, and the Holy Roman Empire was dissolved in August.",
 fact="Napoleon told his soldiers it would be enough to say 'I was at Austerlitz' for people to reply 'There is a brave man'."),
"eu180662": dict(
 bluf="Emperor Napoleon wanted the German states bound to France rather than to Vienna; Austrian Emperor Francis II wanted to keep the imperial title from passing to a French client. Napoleon formed sixteen states into the Confederation of the Rhine in July 1806, and on 6 August Francis laid down the title of Holy Roman Emperor and dissolved an empire about a thousand years old. Some 300 German states had become a few dozen, which gave German nationalism a smaller map to work with.",
 now="The consolidation of 1803-06 cut some 300 German states to a few dozen, the map on which Prussia and Austria contended for Germany until 1866.",
 story="Austrian Emperor Francis II had taken the title Emperor of Austria in 1804 as insurance. After Austerlitz Napoleon grouped sixteen German states, Bavaria and Württemberg among them, into the Confederation of the Rhine under his protection on 12 July 1806, and they declared their secession from the empire on 1 August. On 6 August 1806 Francis laid down the title of Holy Roman Emperor, released the estates from their oaths and dissolved an empire that had lasted about a thousand years.",
 why="Napoleon's reorganisation of western Germany had left the empire without members or purpose. Francis abdicated rather than let the title pass to Napoleon.",
 then="The German states merged into fewer and larger ones, which the Congress of Vienna confirmed as a German Confederation of 39 members in 1815. Francis ruled as Emperor of Austria until 1835.",
 fact="French writer Voltaire had written in 1756 that the empire was neither holy, nor Roman, nor an empire."),
"eu180763": dict(
 bluf="Abolitionists led in Parliament by MP William Wilberforce and in the country by campaigner Thomas Clarkson wanted the British Atlantic slave trade ended by law; Prime Minister Lord Grenville's ministry put the bill through. The Slave Trade Act passed in March 1807, and the Royal Navy then patrolled the West African coast to suppress other nations' trade. The largest carrier had left the trade and turned against it, while slavery itself continued in the British colonies until 1834 and in the Americas for decades.",
 now="The ban turned the largest slave-trading power into the trade's policeman, and British treaties and naval patrols drove the Atlantic trade down over the next sixty years; Freetown's Krio people descend from the Africans landed there.",
 story="Britain carried more enslaved Africans across the Atlantic than any other nation in the 1700s. After 20 years of campaigning by abolitionists including MP William Wilberforce and formerly enslaved writer Olaudah Equiano, Parliament passed the Slave Trade Act in 1807 under Prime Minister Lord Grenville. The Royal Navy's West Africa Squadron then patrolled the coast from Senegal to Angola; liberated captives were landed at Freetown in Sierra Leone.",
 why="Twenty years of campaigning had turned opinion. The war with France had made the trade seem less vital, and an act of 1806 banning the supply of slaves to foreign colonies had already cut it.",
 then="The West Africa Squadron freed about 150,000 people from slave ships over the next sixty years. Slavery itself was abolished in the British colonies by the act of 1833.",
 fact="The United States banned the import of enslaved people in the same year, with effect from 1 January 1808."),
"d-luddites": dict(
 bluf="Framework knitters in Nottinghamshire, then croppers in Yorkshire and weavers in Lancashire, smashed the machines that cut their wages and let the unskilled replace them, to force employers back to the old rates. The government sent over 12,000 troops, made machine-breaking a capital crime in 1812 and hanged or transported dozens by 1813. The machines spread anyway and wages in the hand trades kept falling; the costs of industrialisation fell on workers with no vote.",
 now="'Luddite' now means anyone opposed to new technology, and debates over automation and AI revive their question of who bears the costs of change.",
 story="Wages in the hand trades were falling and food prices were high in the war years. From March 1811 framework knitters in Nottinghamshire broke stocking frames at night, signing letters from a mythical 'General Ludd'. Croppers in Yorkshire and weavers in Lancashire followed in 1812. The government sent over 12,000 troops and made machine-breaking a capital crime.",
 why="Falling wages and high food prices during the Napoleonic Wars came first. Machines that let unskilled workers replace them came second.",
 then="Dozens were hanged or transported to Australia by 1813. The machines spread anyway, and wages in the hand trades kept falling.",
 fact="Poet Lord Byron's maiden speech in the House of Lords, in February 1812, opposed the death penalty for frame-breaking."),
"d-borodino": dict(
 bluf="Russian commander Mikhail Kutuzov, under pressure from the tsar and the army to stop retreating, stood on prepared ground west of Moscow; Emperor Napoleon wanted the decisive battle that would end the war. On 7 September 1812 about 250,000 men fought and some 70,000 were killed or wounded, the bloodiest day of the Napoleonic Wars. Napoleon took the field and the road to Moscow; the Russian army withdrew intact, and the war went on.",
 now="Russia remembers Borodino as a symbol of national resistance to invaders, invoked again in 1941 and in today's politics.",
 story="The Russian armies had retreated since June 1812, trading space for time. Russian commander Mikhail Kutuzov, appointed in August, chose to stand at Borodino. On 7 September 1812 about 250,000 men fought west of Moscow, and some 70,000 were killed or wounded. Kutuzov withdrew in good order, and Napoleon entered Moscow a week later.",
 why="Under pressure to defend Moscow, Kutuzov made a stand on prepared ground rather than continuing to retreat. Napoleon needed a decisive battle before winter.",
 then="Moscow burned, Russian Tsar Alexander I refused to negotiate, and in October the French began their retreat.",
 fact="Napoleon kept the Imperial Guard out of the battle, refusing to risk his last reserve so far from France."),
"eu181264": dict(
 bluf="Emperor Napoleon invaded Russia to force Tsar Alexander I back into the Continental System, his blockade of British trade. He took Moscow in September with an army that had crossed the Niemen about 600,000 strong; the Russians traded space, the city burned and the tsar refused to negotiate, and fewer than 100,000 men came back. The Grande Armée and the aura of invincibility were gone, Prussia and Austria changed sides, and Russia became the dominant land power in Europe for a generation.",
 now="Russia's strategy of trading space for time, and the winter, entered its national memory; Hitler's invasion of 1941 repeated Napoleon's errors of distance and supply.",
 story="In June 1812 Emperor Napoleon crossed the Niemen into Russia with about 600,000 men. The Russian armies retreated, burning crops and villages. After Borodino on 7 September he took Moscow; the city burned and Russian Tsar Alexander I refused to negotiate. The retreat from October, harried by Cossacks and then by winter, destroyed the army; fewer than 100,000 came out.",
 why="Russia had left the Continental System and reopened trade with Britain in 1810, and Napoleon meant to force the tsar back into line.",
 then="Prussia joined Russia in February 1813 and Austria in August. Napoleon was beaten at Leipzig in October 1813 and abdicated in April 1814.",
 fact="About 600,000 men went in and fewer than 100,000 came out; French engineer Charles Minard's 1869 chart of the army's shrinking strength is often called the best statistical graphic ever drawn."),
"d-leipzig": dict(
 bluf="Russia, Prussia, Austria and Sweden combined to drive Emperor Napoleon out of Germany. Over four days in October 1813 about half a million soldiers fought around Leipzig, the largest battle in Europe before 1914; Napoleon was beaten and fell back across the Rhine. His German allies changed sides, the Confederation of the Rhine collapsed, and the allied invasion of France that followed ended his rule in April 1814.",
 now="Leipzig broke Napoleon's hold on Germany and made German national feeling a political force; the states that fought him under Prussian and Austrian command were reorganised at Vienna into the Confederation that held until 1866.",
 story="After the Russian disaster Prussia and Austria joined Russia and Sweden against Napoleon. From 16 to 19 October 1813 about half a million soldiers fought around Leipzig under Austrian Field Marshal Karl Philipp Schwarzenberg's overall allied command, the largest battle in Europe before the First World War. Saxon troops changed sides during the battle. Napoleon was beaten and fell back to France.",
 why="The Russian disaster had cost Napoleon his army and his reputation. Prussia joined Russia in February 1813 and Austria in August, and the allies agreed to fight him only when their combined armies could converge.",
 then="Napoleon's German allies changed sides and the Confederation of the Rhine collapsed. The allies invaded France in January 1814 and Napoleon abdicated in April.",
 fact="The Monument to the Battle of the Nations, unveiled for the centenary in 1913, stands 91 m high."),
"d-elba": dict(
 bluf="With Paris in allied hands and his marshals refusing to fight on, Emperor Napoleon abdicated on 6 April 1814; the allies wanted him out of France and the Bourbons back. The Treaty of Fontainebleau gave him the island of Elba to rule, and French King Louis XVIII took the throne. Elba lay three days' sail from France, and within eleven months Napoleon had escaped and ruled again for a hundred days.",
 now="The escape forced the allies to send Napoleon to St Helena in the South Atlantic in 1815 and to finish the Vienna settlement with his return in mind; a defeated leader with popular support had shown he could come back.",
 story="The allies entered Paris on 31 March 1814. Emperor Napoleon's marshals, Marshal Michel Ney among them, refused to march on the capital, and he abdicated at Fontainebleau on 6 April. The Treaty of Fontainebleau left him the title of emperor and the island of Elba, with a pension France never paid. French King Louis XVIII, brother of the executed Louis XVI, took the throne.",
 why="The allies had captured Paris and the marshals refused to fight on. The allies and French foreign minister Talleyrand saw the Bourbons as the settlement least likely to restart the war.",
 then="Napoleon left Elba on 26 February 1815 and landed in France on 1 March. He ruled for a hundred days until Waterloo.",
 fact="Napoleon tried to poison himself at Fontainebleau the night after abdicating; the poison had lost its strength."),
"eu181565": dict(
 bluf="Emperor Napoleon struck into Belgium to beat the British and Prussian armies separately before the Austrians and Russians arrived; British commander the Duke of Wellington and Prussian Field Marshal Gebhard von Blücher meant to hold until they could join. On 18 June 1815 Wellington's British, Dutch and German army held its ridge until Blücher's Prussians arrived, and the French army broke. Twenty-three years of war between France and Europe ended, the Vienna settlement held, and no general European war followed for a century.",
 now="The order built after Waterloo kept great-power peace in Europe for a century, and 'meeting your Waterloo' still means final defeat.",
 story="Napoleon escaped Elba and retook France in March 1815; the powers at Vienna declared him an outlaw and raised armies. He crossed into Belgium on 15 June and beat the Prussians at Ligny on 16 June. On 18 June, at Waterloo south of Brussels, British commander the Duke of Wellington's army of British, Dutch and German troops held his attacks through the day. Prussian Field Marshal Gebhard von Blücher's army arrived in the evening and the French broke.",
 why="The powers declared Napoleon an outlaw and raised new armies. He struck first in Belgium to beat the British and Prussians before the Austrians and Russians arrived.",
 then="Napoleon abdicated again on 22 June and was exiled to St Helena, where he died in 1821. The Bourbons returned; France paid an indemnity of 700 million francs and accepted an allied occupation army.",
 fact="Most of the fighting was nearer Mont-Saint-Jean; Wellington named the battle after the village where he spent the night."),
"eu181565b": dict(
 bluf="Austrian Foreign Minister Klemens von Metternich, British Foreign Secretary Lord Castlereagh, Russian Tsar Alexander I and French Foreign Minister Talleyrand set out to redraw Europe so that no power could dominate it again and to contain the principles of 1789. They balanced the great powers and created the Concert of Europe to manage crises together. No general European war followed for a century; the suppression of nationalism and liberal revolution produced the revolts of the 1820s, 1830 and 1848 and the national unifications that remade the settlement after 1859.",
 now="The Vienna system is the standard model of a peace built by rivals: the great powers agreed to meet and manage crises together, which kept Europe without a general war from 1815 to 1914.",
 story="Delegates met in Vienna from September 1814, and the Final Act was signed on 9 June 1815, nine days before Waterloo. France kept roughly its borders of 1792; Prussia gained the Rhineland and part of Saxony; Russia took most of Poland; Belgium was joined to the Netherlands; a German Confederation of 39 states replaced the Holy Roman Empire. The Concert of Europe that followed held congresses to manage crises together.",
 why="The powers wanted order restored and no single power able to dominate Europe again. They wanted the revolutionary principle of 1789 contained.",
 then="Conservative powers cooperated to crush liberal and nationalist revolts in Naples in 1821 and Spain in 1823. The settlement eroded with the revolutions of 1848 and the unification of Italy and Germany, and no general war broke out until 1914.",
 fact="The Congress never met in full session; business was done in committees and at balls, and the Prince de Ligne quipped that the Congress danced rather than walked."),
"d-ricardo": dict(
 bluf="London stockbroker David Ricardo wanted to show why the Corn Laws, which taxed imported grain to protect landowners, cost the nation. In 'On the Principles of Political Economy and Taxation' of 1817 he showed with English cloth and Portuguese wine that even a country better at producing everything gains by specialising in what it does relatively best and trading for the rest. Comparative advantage became the core case for free trade, armed the campaign that repealed the Corn Laws in 1846 and remains the argument every tariff debate turns on.",
 now="Comparative advantage is still the core argument for free trade, and today's tariff debates turn on how its gains and losses are shared within countries.",
 story="Britain was arguing over the Corn Law of 1815, which kept out foreign grain until the home price was high. London stockbroker David Ricardo, who had made a fortune on the exchange, published 'On the Principles of Political Economy and Taxation' in 1817. He used English cloth and Portuguese wine to show that each country gains by specialising in what it produces relatively best, even when one produces both goods more cheaply.",
 why="The Corn Laws raised the price of bread and protected landowners, and Ricardo set out to show why free trade benefited the nation. Rent, he argued, went to landowners at the expense of profit and wages.",
 then="His argument armed the Anti-Corn Law League. The Corn Laws were repealed in 1846, and free trade was British policy for 80 years.",
 fact="Ricardo retired from the Stock Exchange at 42 with a fortune and bought a seat in Parliament in 1819."),
"eu182166": dict(
 bluf="Greek rebels rose in March 1821 to throw off Ottoman rule and found a nation on the model of 1789 and the classical past; the sultan sent Egyptian troops to crush them. By 1825 the revolt was failing, until public sympathy and European volunteers moved Britain, France and Russia to intervene, and their fleet sank the Ottoman-Egyptian navy at Navarino in 1827. Greece became independent in 1830, the first state carved from Ottoman Europe, and the mix of national revolt and great-power intervention became the pattern for Serbia, Romania and Bulgaria.",
 now="Its mix of nationalist revolt and great-power intervention became the pattern for Serbia, Romania and Bulgaria, and the Greek-Turkish rivalry it began still shapes the eastern Mediterranean.",
 story="Greek rebels rose against Ottoman rule in March 1821. Both sides massacred civilians: Greeks killed the Muslims of Tripolitsa in 1821 and Ottoman forces killed or enslaved most of the population of Chios in 1822. By 1825 Egyptian troops under Ibrahim Pasha, sent by the sultan, were crushing the revolt. European volunteers such as English poet Lord Byron, who died at Missolonghi in 1824, and public sympathy pushed Britain, France and Russia to intervene; their fleet destroyed the Ottoman-Egyptian navy at Navarino on 20 October 1827.",
 why="Greek nationalism, fed by the French Revolution and the classical heritage, against Ottoman rule. The Orthodox merchant diaspora and the secret society Filiki Eteria organised the rising.",
 then="Greece became independent by the London Protocol of 1830, the first new state from the Ottoman Empire, with Bavarian prince Otto as king from 1832.",
 fact="Navarino was the last major battle fought entirely by sailing ships."),
"d-decembrist": dict(
 bluf="Reform-minded officers of the Russian guards wanted a constitution and an end to serfdom, and used the confused succession after Tsar Alexander I's death to try to force them. On 26 December 1825 about 3,000 soldiers on Senate Square refused the oath to Nicholas I; cannon scattered them, five leaders were hanged and more than 100 were exiled to Siberia. Russian Tsar Nicholas I ruled as a strict autocrat for 30 years with censorship and a secret police, and the Decembrists became the martyrs of every later Russian revolutionary movement.",
 now="The revolt fixed Nicholas I's choice of police rule: the Third Section, his secret police, dates from 1826, and the Decembrists' example gave every Russian revolutionary movement to 1917 its founding martyrs.",
 story="Tsar Alexander I died in December 1825, and his brother Constantine, the heir, had secretly renounced the throne in favour of the younger Nicholas. On 26 December 1825, 14 December in Russia's calendar, about 3,000 soldiers led by reformist officers refused to swear loyalty to Nicholas I and gathered on Senate Square in St Petersburg. Cannon fire scattered them. Five leaders, among them poet Kondraty Ryleyev and Colonel Pavel Pestel, were hanged, and more than 100 were exiled to Siberia.",
 why="Officers who had seen Western Europe in the Napoleonic Wars wanted a constitution and an end to serfdom. The confusion over the succession gave them an opening.",
 then="Russian Tsar Nicholas I ruled as a strict autocrat for 30 years, with censorship and the Third Section secret police from 1826. Serfdom lasted until 1861.",
 fact="Three of the five ropes broke at the hanging, and the men were hanged a second time."),
"eu182567": dict(
 bluf="Durham colliery owners wanted a cheap way to move coal from the pits to the Tees, and engineer George Stephenson wanted to show that steam locomotives could do the work. On 27 September 1825 Stephenson drove Locomotion No. 1 along the Stockton and Darlington Railway with wagons of coal and passengers, the first public railway to use steam. Railways cut transport costs, created national markets and standard time, and within 25 years Britain had over 6,000 miles of track.",
 now="Railways created standard time and national markets, and rail remains central to moving freight and people, from Chinese high-speed lines to European freight corridors.",
 story="Colliery owners in County Durham needed to move coal from the pits to the River Tees. Quaker banker Edward Pease backed the line and engineer George Stephenson built it. On 27 September 1825 Stephenson drove Locomotion No. 1 along the line pulling wagons of coal and passengers. It was the first public railway to use steam locomotives; horses worked much of its traffic for years.",
 why="Mine owners wanted a cheap way to move coal from the Durham pits to the River Tees. Stephenson had built colliery locomotives since 1814.",
 then="The Liverpool and Manchester line of 1830 proved the intercity passenger railway. Within 25 years Britain had over 6,000 miles of track, and railway building spread across Europe and North America.",
 fact="A man on horseback carrying a flag rode ahead of the train on the opening day."),
"d-liverpool": dict(
 bluf="Manchester's cotton mills and Liverpool's merchants wanted a fast, cheap link between the port and the mills to break the canal companies' hold. The line that engineers George and Robert Stephenson built opened on 15 September 1830 with timetabled steam trains for passengers and freight, the first modern intercity railway. It carried hundreds of thousands of passengers in its first year, proved that railways could pay, and set off the railway booms of Britain and the world.",
 now="Stephenson's track gauge of 4 ft 8½ in became the world standard, a classic case of an early technical choice locking in for centuries.",
 story="The line opened on 15 September 1830 with Prime Minister the Duke of Wellington aboard. At Parkside the Liverpool MP William Huskisson stepped onto the track and was struck by the Stephensons' Rocket; he died that evening, one of the first railway deaths. Within months the line was carrying hundreds of thousands of passengers a year.",
 why="Manchester's cotton mills needed fast, cheap links to the port of Liverpool. The canals charged monopoly rates.",
 then="Britain's 'railway mania' of the 1840s followed, as investors poured money into thousands of miles of track.",
 fact="The Rocket won the Rainhill Trials of 1829, held to decide whether locomotives or fixed engines would work the line."),
"eu183068": dict(
 bluf="French King Charles X issued ordinances in July 1830 dissolving the chamber, cutting the electorate and muzzling the press to restore royal authority; Parisians built barricades to stop him. After three days of fighting he fled, and the liberal Duke of Orléans took the throne as Louis-Philippe, 'King of the French'. The Vienna settlement had failed to freeze Europe, and revolts followed in Belgium, Poland and Italy within months.",
 now="It showed a revolution could change a regime without ending monarchy; Delacroix's 'Liberty Leading the People' became an emblem of democratic revolution.",
 story="French King Charles X, on the throne since 1824, issued four ordinances on 25 July 1830 that dissolved the newly elected chamber, restricted the vote and muzzled the press. Parisians built barricades and fought the troops from 27 to 29 July, the 'Three Glorious Days'. Charles fled to England. The liberal Duke of Orléans became Louis-Philippe, 'King of the French', on 9 August.",
 why="Charles X tried to restore royal absolutism after liberals won the elections of 1830. Unemployment and dear bread in Paris supplied the crowds.",
 then="Belgium rose in August 1830, Poland in November and Italy in 1831. Louis-Philippe's regime rested on the bankers and businessmen who were the only people with the vote.",
 fact="Charles X was the last king of France crowned at Reims, in 1825."),
"eu183068b": dict(
 bluf="Catholic and largely French-speaking Belgians wanted out of the Protestant, Dutch-run kingdom the Congress of Vienna had created; Dutch King William I wanted it kept by force. A riot after an opera in Brussels in August 1830 spread into revolt, the Dutch army failed to retake the city in September, and the great powers recognised Belgian independence and in 1839 guaranteed its neutrality. The map of 1815 had been changed by revolt and accepted by the powers, and the guarantee brought Britain into war in 1914.",
 now="The 1839 guarantee of neutrality made Belgium the trigger of Britain's entry into the war of 1914; the divide between Dutch and French speakers that the new state inherited still shapes its politics.",
 story="The Congress of Vienna had joined Belgium to the Netherlands under Dutch King William I. On 25 August 1830 a performance of the opera 'La Muette de Portici' in Brussels ended in riot, and revolt spread across the south. Dutch troops failed to retake Brussels in September, and a provisional government declared independence in October. The great powers recognised Belgium at the London Conference of 1831, Leopold of Saxe-Coburg became king, and in 1839 the Treaty of London guaranteed its neutrality.",
 why="Catholic, largely French-speaking Belgians resented the Protestant king, rules favouring the Dutch language, and taxes and policies that served the north. The July Revolution in Paris a month earlier showed a king could be removed.",
 then="The 1839 guarantee was the 'scrap of paper' that brought Britain into war when Germany invaded Belgium in 1914.",
 fact="The revolt began in the opera house during a duet about love of country."),
"d-factoryact": dict(
 bluf="Evangelical reformers led by Tory MP Lord Ashley and the factory workers' short-time committees wanted a ten-hour day for all mill workers; the Whig government offered a narrower law. The Factory Act of 1833 banned children under 9 from textile mills, limited 9- to 13-year-olds to 9 hours a day and 13- to 18-year-olds to 12, and created inspectors to enforce it. The state had begun to regulate industrial work to protect the vulnerable, and factory inspection became the model for workplace regulation worldwide.",
 now="Factory inspection became a model for workplace regulation around the world.",
 story="A parliamentary committee of 1832 under Tory MP Michael Sadler took evidence of children working 12 to 16 hours a day in mills. Tory MP Lord Ashley took up the ten-hour bill after Sadler lost his seat. The Act of 1833 banned children under 9 from textile mills, limited 9- to 13-year-olds to 9 hours a day and 13- to 18-year-olds to 12, required schooling for the younger children, and created the first factory inspectors.",
 why="The inquiries exposed the hours children worked. Evangelical reformers and the workers' short-time committees campaigned for limits.",
 then="The Ten Hours Act of 1847 extended protection to women and young people. Factory regulation spread to other industrial countries.",
 fact="Four inspectors were appointed to cover every textile mill in Britain."),
"eu183369": dict(
 bluf="Abolitionists led in the Commons by MP Thomas Fowell Buxton wanted slavery ended across the empire; Prime Minister Earl Grey's Whig government wanted it done without ruining the planters. The Slavery Abolition Act of 1833 freed about 800,000 people from 1 August 1834, paid £20 million, about 40 per cent of the national budget, to the owners, and bound the freed to years of unpaid 'apprenticeship'. The world's leading power became an opponent of slavery, and the terms of its abolition remain a grievance.",
 now="British taxpayers finished repaying the loan for that compensation only in 2015, and Caribbean governments cite this history in their demands for reparations.",
 story="A revolt of tens of thousands of enslaved people in Jamaica at Christmas 1831, led by Baptist deacon Samuel Sharpe, was crushed with hundreds of executions. The reformed Parliament elected in 1832 passed the Slavery Abolition Act in August 1833 under Prime Minister Earl Grey. It paid £20 million, about 40 per cent of the national budget, to slave owners and nothing to the enslaved. Freedom came on 1 August 1834 with a period of apprenticeship.",
 why="Decades of abolitionist campaigning came first. The Jamaican revolt of 1831 showed the cost of holding on. The reformed Parliament of 1832 had fewer seats in the planters' hands.",
 then="Protests ended apprenticeship early, in 1838, and Caribbean sugar declined. Britain used its navy and diplomacy to press other countries to end slavery, and the owners' compensation was invested in Britain.",
 fact="The £20 million was raised by a loan from banker Nathan Rothschild and his brother-in-law Moses Montefiore."),
"d-zollverein": dict(
 bluf="Prussian Finance Minister Friedrich von Motz set out to join Prussia's two blocks of territory under one tariff and to draw the small states between them into it, with Austria kept out. The Zollverein came into force on 1 January 1834 with eighteen states and about 23 million people trading on one external tariff. The German economy was united under Prussia thirty-seven years before the German state, and the union decided which of the two German powers would do the uniting.",
 now="It is the standard case for economic integration preceding political union, cited in accounts of the EEC's founding and in arguments about whether a currency union needs a state.",
 story="On 1 January 1834 the Zollverein came into force, abolishing internal tariffs among eighteen states with about 23 million people. Prussia had built it by absorbing the small states inside its borders after 1818 and then outbidding Austria, which stayed out. Goods moved on one tariff; railways, which began in Germany the following year, followed the customs lines.",
 why="Prussia's lands after 1815 lay in two blocks with small states between them, so its 1818 tariff needed neighbours inside it. Austria's protected industries could not survive a common tariff, so Vienna stayed out.",
 trig="The treaty took effect on 1 January 1834, joining eighteen states and about 23 million people under one external tariff.",
 then="Austria's attempts to join in 1849-53 failed. By the 1860s the member states traded mainly with Prussia, and the union survived the Austro-Prussian war of 1866 intact.",
 fact="The Zollverein issued a common silver coin, the Vereinsthaler, from 1857."),
"eu184570": dict(
 bluf="Potato blight destroyed the crop a third of Ireland lived on, in 1845 and the years after; British Prime Ministers Robert Peel and then Lord John Russell set out to relieve the hunger without disturbing markets or landlords. Relief was slow and limited while grain and cattle still left the country; about a million died and a million emigrated. Ireland's population fell by a quarter and kept falling for a century, and Irish nationalism took the famine as its charge against British rule.",
 now="The famine made emigration Ireland's demographic norm for a century and gave Irish nationalism, at home and among the diaspora, its charge against British rule; over 30 million Americans claim Irish ancestry.",
 story="Blight destroyed the potato, the main food of Irish peasants, in 1845, almost completely in 1846 and again in 1848. British Prime Minister Robert Peel bought maize for relief in 1845-46; Lord John Russell's government from 1846 relied on public works, then soup kitchens, then the poor law. Ireland kept exporting grain and cattle to England throughout. About a million died of hunger and disease, and about a million emigrated.",
 why="Over a third of the population depended almost entirely on the potato, grown on tiny plots rented from often absent landlords. Blight destroyed the crop repeatedly from 1845. The British government, holding to free-market principles, limited relief and let food exports continue.",
 then="Ireland's population fell by about a quarter and kept shrinking until the 20th century, as mass emigration to the United States, Britain and Australia continued. Bitterness toward British rule strengthened nationalist movements.",
 fact="In 1847 the Choctaw Nation, forced down the Trail of Tears sixteen years earlier, sent money to help starving Irish families."),
"d-cornlaws": dict(
 bluf="Manchester manufacturer Richard Cobden and Quaker manufacturer John Bright's Anti-Corn Law League wanted the tariffs on imported grain that protected landowners repealed; Prime Minister Robert Peel came to agree, and the Irish famine gave him the occasion. Repeal passed in June 1846 with opposition votes and split his Conservative Party, which turned him out within days. Britain had shifted from protecting landowners to free trade serving industry and consumers, and stayed its champion for 80 years.",
 now="It remains the textbook case of trade politics as a contest between interests, and today's tariff debates repeat its arguments about who gains and who pays.",
 story="The Corn Laws of 1815 taxed imported grain to protect landowners. The Anti-Corn Law League, founded in Manchester in 1838 by manufacturer Richard Cobden and Quaker manufacturer John Bright, campaigned with pamphlets, lectures and voter registration. With famine in Ireland, Conservative Prime Minister Robert Peel carried repeal in 1846 with Whig votes, and his own party split.",
 why="Industrial towns wanted cheap bread and manufacturers wanted foreign markets. Free-trade ideas had spread since Adam Smith. The Irish famine made a tax on food indefensible.",
 then="Peel's government fell within days of repeal, and the Conservatives had no majority again for 28 years. Britain led an era of free trade; cheap grain imports from the 1870s hit British farming.",
 fact="The Conservative split put the Peelites, among them William Gladstone, on the road to the Liberal Party."),
"d-frankfurt": dict(
 bluf="Delegates elected across the German states in the spring of 1848 met in Frankfurt to unite Germany as a liberal constitutional state by consent. They wrote a constitution with a list of basic rights and in April 1849 offered the crown to Prussian King Frederick William IV, who refused 'a crown from the gutter'; troops dispersed the remnant in June. Unification by elected assembly had failed, and Germany was united instead by Prussian arms in 1871 under an emperor and a parliament of limited power.",
 now="Germany's black-red-gold flag and its post-war Basic Law draw on the Frankfurt Parliament.",
 story="The March revolutions forced the German princes to allow elections. The assembly opened in St Paul's Church, Frankfurt, on 18 May 1848 and wrote a liberal constitution with a list of basic rights. In March 1849 it adopted the constitution and offered the crown of a united Germany to Prussian King Frederick William IV, who refused 'a crown from the gutter'. Troops dispersed the remnant at Stuttgart in June 1849.",
 why="Revolutions across Europe had forced the German princes to allow elections. The assembly had no army and no money of its own and depended on Prussia and Austria accepting its decisions.",
 then="Prussian Minister-President Otto von Bismarck united Germany by war in 1871, under an emperor with a parliament of limited power. Many liberals emigrated, some to the United States.",
 fact="The assembly argued for months over whether Austria belonged in Germany, the 'greater' against the 'smaller' German solution."),
"eu184871": dict(
 bluf="Liberals, nationalists and workers across Europe rose in 1848 to win constitutions, national unity and relief from slump, after Paris overthrew King Louis-Philippe in February. Within a year the armies of Austria, Prussia and Russia and the French republic's own troops had crushed most of the risings. Serfdom ended in the Habsburg lands, Prussia got a constitution, France got universal male suffrage and then a Bonaparte, and nationalism became the dominant political force; unification would come by Prussian and Piedmontese arms rather than liberal parliaments.",
 now="'1848' is the template for sudden waves of revolt, such as 1989 or the Arab Spring.",
 story="Bad harvests and an economic slump had left Europe's cities hungry and unemployed. Paris rose in February 1848 and King Louis-Philippe fled; Vienna, Berlin, Milan, Budapest and dozens of other cities followed in March, demanding constitutions, press freedom and national unity. Austrian Chancellor Klemens von Metternich fled Vienna. Most of the risings were crushed within a year.",
 why="Bad harvests, including the potato blight, an economic slump and unemployment came first. Decades of liberal and nationalist frustration with the conservative order of 1815 came second.",
 trig="The February revolution in Paris, which overthrew King Louis-Philippe.",
 then="Austria abolished serfdom, Prussia got a constitution, and France got universal male suffrage, then elected Louis-Napoleon Bonaparte president in December 1848; he made himself emperor in 1852. Conservative forces regrouped, and unification came later through Prussian and Piedmontese power politics rather than liberal parliaments.",
 fact="1848 is called the 'Springtime of Peoples'; risings hit more than 50 places in a few months."),
"eu184871b": dict(
 bluf="German philosopher Karl Marx and his collaborator Friedrich Engels wrote a programme for the Communist League, a small group of radical workers, to explain history as class struggle and to forecast the overthrow of capitalism by the industrial working class. Published in German in London in February 1848, it had little effect on the revolutions of that year. Its doctrine became that of the mass socialist parties after 1870 and, after 1917, of states that ruled a third of humanity.",
 now="Regimes claiming Marx's inspiration once ruled a third of humanity, and the Manifesto remains one of the most widely read political texts ever written.",
 story="The Communist League, a small group of radical workers, commissioned a programme from German philosopher Karl Marx and his collaborator Friedrich Engels in late 1847. The pamphlet argued that history is a story of class struggle, that the bourgeoisie had created the proletariat that would overthrow it, and that the workers had nothing to lose but their chains. It was published in German in London in February 1848, days before the revolution in Paris. It had little effect at first.",
 why="Industrialisation had created an urban working class in harsh conditions. Capitalism's booms and busts were plain to see. The revolutionary climate of 1848 made radical change seem possible.",
 then="Marx's later work, above all 'Das Kapital' of 1867, shaped socialist and labour movements. The Russian Revolution of 1917 put Marxists in power; reformist socialists turned to democratic politics and the welfare state.",
 fact="The first English translation appeared in 1850 in a Chartist newspaper, the Red Republican."),
}
